  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nOutside of valley fog this morning, quiet and dry weather will\npersist through sunset under high pressure.\n\nA cold front currently moving across the Great Lakes will\ncontinue advancing into the Ohio River Valley over the next 24\nhours. Limited moisture along the boundary will keep\nprecipitation chances low, with only spotty light rain early\nFriday morning. Rainfall amounts will generally range from a\ntrace to a tenth of an inch north of I-70 and along the ridges.\nDrier conditions will return late Friday morning, although\nclouds may linger. Despite the frontal passage, temperatures\nwill remain slightly above average.\n\nA weak disturbance is expected to move through the region late\nSunday into Monday. However, weak forcing and limited moisture\nshould keep rainfall totals below 0.25 inches. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nEnsemble guidance continues to indicate the development of a\nclosed 500-mb low over the central CONUS Tuesday into Wednesday,\nwith the potential for the system to eject northeastward toward\nthe Great Lakes thereafter. Considerable uncertainty remains\nregarding the track and strength of this disturbance late into\nthe week. If the system tracks near the region, wetting rainfall\nand breezy conditions would be the most likely impacts. However,\nthe overall risk of flooding remains low, with the probabilities\nbelow 30%, given our recent dry stretch.",
